  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Friday...\n\nSeasonably warm and dry conditions exist today under a ridge of \nhigh pressure aloft. Over and near the mountains and foothills, \nthe combination of stronger winds aloft and a favorable surface \npressure gradient is resulting in breezy to windy conditions, \nespecially over the foothills west of Billings (Livingston to Nye \nand Big Timber to Harlowton). This morning, peak wind gusts in the\n40s and 50s (mph) were common in these areas. Look for winds to \nremain breezy this afternoon, while gradually subsiding into this \nevening. Tonight, a dry cold front passage will end any remaining \nwindy conditions across the region.\n\nSunday will be a slightly cooler day with high temperatures in \nthe 60s for most. Winds will be lighter but still breezy out of \nthe east at times. \n\nMonday will see seasonably warm conditions return as a ridge of \nhigh pressure nudges back into the region. Over the plains of \neastern Montana (generally east of Rosebud County), breezy \nsoutheasterly winds are expected to develop late Sunday night, \npeaking during the day Monday when wind gusts in the 20s and 30s \n(mph) are forecast. \n\nTuesday into the second half of the week look to remain \nseasonably warm and mainly dry, though a developing low pressure \nsystem off the west coast of the United States may occasionally \neject energy and moisture into our southern mountains, allowing an\noccasional slight chance of precipitation. By late next week, \nthis low pressure system is expected to move onshore. How exactly \nthis occurs remains uncertain. Therefore, while this weather \nsystem could impact our region late next week, a lot of \nuncertainty remains. LA",
